WEBVTT

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:03.371
Here's chlorine on the periodic table: thirty-five point

00:00:03.451 --> 00:00:03.972
four five.

00:00:04.514 --> 00:00:06.415
But we just saw that chlorine atoms come

00:00:06.536 --> 00:00:09.118
as chlorine-thirty-five and chlorine-thirty-seven.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.224
Not one of them has a mass of

00:00:11.285 --> 00:00:12.609
thirty-five point four five.

00:00:13.473 --> 00:00:15.283
And it isn't the simple average either.

00:00:15.907 --> 00:00:18.837
Halfway between thirty-five and thirty-seven is thirty-six.

00:00:19.483 --> 00:00:21.758
So where does thirty-five point four five come

00:00:21.819 --> 00:00:21.979
from?

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.689
Think about a real sample of chlorine.

00:00:25.292 --> 00:00:27.504
It's a mixture of both isotopes, and they're

00:00:27.565 --> 00:00:28.469
not equally common.

00:00:29.173 --> 00:00:31.996
Out of every hundred chlorine atoms, about seventy-six

00:00:32.017 --> 00:00:36.023
are chlorine-thirty-five, and only about twenty-four are chlorine-thirty-seven.

00:00:36.725 --> 00:00:38.368
So to find the average mass of one

00:00:38.448 --> 00:00:40.632
atom, add up the masses of all hundred,

00:00:41.013 --> 00:00:42.435
then share the total among them.

00:00:43.298 --> 00:00:46.342
Seventy-six atoms at thirty-five, plus twenty-four atoms at

00:00:46.383 --> 00:00:49.587
thirty-seven, gives three thousand five hundred and forty-eight.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.776
Divide by a hundred atoms, and that's thirty-five

00:00:52.837 --> 00:00:53.559
point four eight.

00:00:54.281 --> 00:00:56.585
It's much closer to thirty-five, because most of

00:00:56.607 --> 00:00:58.149
the atoms are chlorine-thirty-five.

00:00:59.098 --> 00:01:00.428
That's a weighted average.

00:01:01.014 --> 00:01:03.598
Each isotope's mass counts as many times as

00:01:03.639 --> 00:01:06.424
that isotope occurs, so the common isotope counts

00:01:06.485 --> 00:01:06.664
more.

00:01:07.608 --> 00:01:10.553
Relative atomic mass, written A r, is this

00:01:10.674 --> 00:01:12.658
weighted average for the atoms in a natural

00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:14.722
sample of an element, on the scale where

00:01:14.763 --> 00:01:16.406
carbon-twelve is exactly twelve.

00:01:17.189 --> 00:01:19.346
Like any relative mass, it has no units.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.154
The percentages, called abundances, are measured with an

00:01:23.215 --> 00:01:26.178
instrument called a mass spectrometer, which sorts atoms

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:26.699
by mass.

00:01:27.402 --> 00:01:28.869
How it works is a story for later.

00:01:29.232 --> 00:01:31.019
Here, the abundances are simply given.

00:01:31.823 --> 00:01:33.859
In questions, you'll use a shortcut.

00:01:34.342 --> 00:01:36.949
Turn each percentage into a fraction by dividing

00:01:36.989 --> 00:01:37.651
by a hundred,

00:01:38.293 --> 00:01:41.683
multiply each isotope's mass by its fraction, then

00:01:41.703 --> 00:01:42.304
add them up.

00:01:42.946 --> 00:01:46.192
For chlorine, the abundances are seventy-five point seven

00:01:46.232 --> 00:01:49.277
seven percent, and twenty-four point two three percent.

00:01:49.919 --> 00:01:52.950
Thirty-five times zero point seven five seven seven

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:55.019
is twenty-six point five two.

00:01:55.662 --> 00:01:58.334
Thirty-seven times zero point two four two three

00:01:58.637 --> 00:01:59.842
is eight point nine seven.

00:02:00.425 --> 00:02:02.614
Add them, and you get thirty-five point four

00:02:02.696 --> 00:02:04.624
eight, about thirty-five point five.

00:02:05.246 --> 00:02:08.289
The periodic table says thirty-five point four five

00:02:08.471 --> 00:02:11.394
because it uses each isotope's exact mass, which

00:02:11.475 --> 00:02:13.998
is slightly less than thirty-five or thirty-seven.

00:02:14.772 --> 00:02:15.581
Here's a way to picture it.

00:02:16.088 --> 00:02:18.356
Put the isotopes on a beam, at their

00:02:18.397 --> 00:02:21.227
masses, and stack each one by how common

00:02:21.248 --> 00:02:21.528
it is.

00:02:22.172 --> 00:02:24.505
The relative atomic mass is where the beam

00:02:24.526 --> 00:02:25.129
would balance.

00:02:25.754 --> 00:02:27.763
With three quarters of the weight at thirty-five,

00:02:28.186 --> 00:02:29.894
the balance point sits close to it.

00:02:30.298 --> 00:02:31.952
The average leans toward the crowd.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:35.334
Quick prediction.

00:02:36.059 --> 00:02:40.205
Boron has two isotopes: boron-ten, at nineteen point

00:02:40.246 --> 00:02:43.551
nine percent, and boron-eleven, at eighty point one

00:02:43.572 --> 00:02:43.911
percent.

00:02:44.433 --> 00:02:46.498
Will its relative atomic mass be closer to

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.324
ten, or closer to eleven?

00:02:48.888 --> 00:02:49.312
Have a think.

00:02:53.009 --> 00:02:55.805
Closer to eleven, because boron-eleven is the crowd.

00:02:56.534 --> 00:02:56.942
Let's check.

00:02:57.709 --> 00:03:00.370
As fractions, the abundances are zero point one

00:03:00.431 --> 00:03:03.172
nine nine, and zero point eight zero one,

00:03:03.713 --> 00:03:05.913
and they add to exactly one, which is

00:03:05.935 --> 00:03:06.594
a good first check.

00:03:07.498 --> 00:03:09.567
Ten times zero point one nine nine

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:11.374
is one point nine nine.

00:03:11.978 --> 00:03:14.122
Eleven times zero point eight zero one

00:03:14.625 --> 00:03:16.228
is eight point eight one one.

00:03:17.031 --> 00:03:19.595
Add them, and A r is ten point

00:03:19.736 --> 00:03:20.557
eight zero one,

00:03:21.079 --> 00:03:23.362
or ten point eight to three significant figures.

00:03:24.269 --> 00:03:26.136
Always sense check your answer.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:29.005
It must land between the lightest and heaviest

00:03:29.186 --> 00:03:31.551
isotopes, and nearer the more common one.

00:03:32.436 --> 00:03:34.282
Ten point eight passes both tests.

00:03:34.946 --> 00:03:37.538
And notice that no single boron atom has

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:38.845
a mass of ten point eight.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.300
Every atom is boron-ten or boron-eleven.

00:03:43.082 --> 00:03:44.943
Ten point eight is the average of the

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:47.466
sample, not the mass number of any atom.

00:03:48.418 --> 00:03:49.729
Now the question in reverse.

00:03:50.335 --> 00:03:52.627
Suppose you know the relative atomic mass, and

00:03:52.668 --> 00:03:53.874
you want the abundances.

00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:57.041
In this question, silicon has a relative atomic

00:03:57.082 --> 00:03:59.084
mass of twenty-eight point two two,

00:03:59.646 --> 00:04:01.668
and we assume it has only two isotopes,

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.992
silicon-twenty-eight and silicon-twenty-nine.

00:04:04.734 --> 00:04:07.341
Real silicon also has some silicon-thirty, so this

00:04:07.422 --> 00:04:08.384
is a simplified model.

00:04:09.128 --> 00:04:11.564
Let x be the fraction of silicon-twenty-eight.

00:04:12.209 --> 00:04:14.477
With only two isotopes, the fractions add to

00:04:14.659 --> 00:04:17.891
one, so silicon-twenty-nine is one minus x.

00:04:18.442 --> 00:04:20.678
Now write the weighted average with x in

00:04:20.738 --> 00:04:20.798
it.

00:04:21.382 --> 00:04:24.910
Twenty-eight x, plus twenty-nine times one minus x,

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:26.814
equals twenty-eight point two two.

00:04:27.575 --> 00:04:29.960
Expand the bracket, and the x terms combine

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.426
to minus x, so twenty-nine minus x equals

00:04:33.567 --> 00:04:34.769
twenty-eight point two two.

00:04:35.492 --> 00:04:37.482
That makes x zero point seven eight.

00:04:37.984 --> 00:04:42.422
So silicon-twenty-eight is seventy-eight percent, and silicon-twenty-nine is

00:04:42.562 --> 00:04:43.486
twenty-two percent.

00:04:44.147 --> 00:04:44.408
Check it:

00:04:44.929 --> 00:04:47.554
twenty-one point eight four plus six point three

00:04:47.594 --> 00:04:50.079
eight gives back twenty-eight point two two.

00:04:50.757 --> 00:04:51.241
Your turn.

00:04:51.826 --> 00:04:54.791
A model element has two isotopes, with masses

00:04:54.912 --> 00:04:56.575
twenty-two and twenty-four,

00:04:57.117 --> 00:05:00.283
and abundances of seventy point zero percent and

00:05:00.444 --> 00:05:01.746
thirty point zero percent.

00:05:02.409 --> 00:05:04.506
Calculate its relative atomic mass.

00:05:05.356 --> 00:05:06.188
Pause here to answer.

00:05:17.008 --> 00:05:19.872
As fractions, the abundances are zero point seven

00:05:19.913 --> 00:05:20.614
zero zero,

00:05:21.256 --> 00:05:22.978
and zero point three zero zero.

00:05:24.001 --> 00:05:26.906
Twenty-two times zero point seven zero zero is

00:05:26.967 --> 00:05:27.967
fifteen point four,

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:31.374
and twenty-four times zero point three zero zero

00:05:31.515 --> 00:05:32.576
is seven point two.

00:05:33.418 --> 00:05:35.546
Together that's twenty-two point six.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:40.138
It's between twenty-two and twenty-four, and nearer twenty-two,

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.622
the more common isotope.

00:05:42.325 --> 00:05:45.261
Twenty-three, the simple average, is the slip to

00:05:45.323 --> 00:05:45.684
avoid.

00:05:47.525 --> 00:05:47.949
Last one.

00:05:48.692 --> 00:05:51.719
Assume an element has only two isotopes, with

00:05:51.759 --> 00:05:55.908
masses sixty-three and sixty-five, and a relative atomic

00:05:55.948 --> 00:05:58.374
mass of sixty-three point six zero.

00:05:58.996 --> 00:06:01.766
Calculate the percentage abundance of each isotope.

00:06:02.511 --> 00:06:03.418
Pause here to answer.

00:06:13.895 --> 00:06:15.905
Let x be the fraction of the mass

00:06:16.067 --> 00:06:17.434
sixty-three isotope.

00:06:18.136 --> 00:06:22.058
Sixty-three x, plus sixty-five times one minus x,

00:06:22.479 --> 00:06:24.499
equals sixty-three point six zero,

00:06:25.001 --> 00:06:28.442
which simplifies to sixty-five minus two x equals

00:06:28.603 --> 00:06:29.823
sixty-three point six zero.

00:06:30.464 --> 00:06:33.665
So x is zero point seven zero: seventy

00:06:33.706 --> 00:06:37.248
percent mass sixty-three, and thirty percent mass sixty-five.

00:06:37.833 --> 00:06:38.423
Don't swap them.

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:42.467
Sixty-three point six is near sixty-three, so sixty-three

00:06:42.608 --> 00:06:43.150
is the crowd.

00:06:45.062 --> 00:06:47.913
So relative atomic mass is all about nuclei,

00:06:48.255 --> 00:06:50.142
and how common each isotope is.

00:06:50.684 --> 00:06:53.372
But remember, isotopes behave the same in reactions,

00:06:53.753 --> 00:06:55.297
because their electrons are the same.

00:06:56.059 --> 00:06:58.568
So what are those electrons actually doing?

00:06:59.392 --> 00:07:01.069
Our best clue comes from light.

00:07:01.635 --> 00:07:04.184
When hydrogen gas glows, it gives out only

00:07:04.245 --> 00:07:06.674
a few sharp colours, not a whole rainbow.

00:07:07.499 --> 00:07:09.388
Next time, we'll see what those colours reveal

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:11.539
about the energy of electrons in an atom.

00:07:14.195 --> 00:07:15.017
Here are the key notes.

00:07:15.541 --> 00:07:17.088
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:17.129 --> 00:07:17.550
to copy them.
